Change(genesis='66602cd0dec37d58d03eec9518ca805a7ac38ea912d2b0c366a0b67b4b9f50b6', deps=('e4dd01681e352187287e653dc1dbf6cdecfe60f98a84dfdf0ac33f125f1d6b16',), event=Revised(decision='versionreading', at=datetime(2026, 11, 17, 0, 0, 0), actor='assistant', title='The version is a reading of the roadmap: a release is a todo of the public roadmap store whose spec composes its items', body="*THE VERSION IS A READING OF THE ROADMAP* (the owner, 2026-10-12, as a reading of the project chain; of the public roadmap store since 2026-11-17; `suzatary_core::release`). A release is a todo of the public roadmap store whose spec composes its items (`Ref`s), declared in order in the `releases.toml` beside that store, which names the store by its genesis, each with its own `major` and `minor`. A deployment finds the plan store as the one mount whose store holds that genesis; with none mounted, no release is cut. A plan whose versions do not strictly increase is refused. MAJOR.MINOR is the last completed release's (`base`.0 before any); PATCH counts the trains since that MAJOR.MINOR was first tagged, from origin/master's newest `Release:` trailer. Items show a release's progress and move no version. `Reading::of` is the one rule, a join over the SET of objects: completed once is completed, so a reopened release stays shipped. The cut is a function of two public inputs, the code tree and one roadmap commit, which the release commit names in its `Tasks:` trailer. A PR names what it completes on `Completes:` lines of its description (AGENTS.md); each id is placed by where it is found, on the roadmap first and then on the upkeep chain, and only a roadmap id counts toward the cut and is named in `app/release.toml` and the public changelog. A land writes three repositories in one order: the code (master and `v<version>` in one atomic push), then core's publication, then the roadmap, in one commit that holds the completions, the pin moved to core's publication and the pictures drawn again, read by the push scan before it is pushed and naming the landed commit in `Shipped:`. Whether each step is done is read from what the repositories hold, so every train command repairs a land cut short by running the same steps (`train.sh record` by hand). A completion written to the roadmap names the release alone and nothing of the private repository; a private todo's completion goes to the upkeep chain with its pull request. The binary that writes is built from a named commit, never the checkout as it is. So the tag, the changelog, `app/release.toml` (the version and the IDS of what it completed, never a title, since the file is public) and the roadmap say one thing, and a refused push writes nothing.", rationale="The owner, 2026-10-12; plain feature releases since 2026-10-23. The tags up to 2.1.2 were read while items counted toward MINOR. Revised 2026-11-17: the project's tasks moved to a public roadmap repository of their own (the owner's decisions of 2026-11-15), so the plan sits beside that store and the land records there.", supersedes=()))